    id: "g8-eng-ch01-b-q08",
    prompt: "Ask your grandparents what the night sky looked like when they were children, and many will describe something you may never have seen: a sky so crowded with stars that the Milky Way stretched across it like a spilled river of salt. Today, in most Indian cities, a child can count the visible stars on two hands.\n\nThe culprit is not cloud or dust alone. It is light, our own light, pouring upward from streetlamps, hoardings and buildings that glow all night whether anyone needs them or not. Scientists call this light pollution, and its effects reach far beyond astronomy. Migrating birds, which navigate partly by the stars, become confused and circle lit towers until they are exhausted. Sea turtle hatchlings on the beaches of Odisha crawl towards bright roads instead of the moonlit sea. Even our own sleep suffers when our bodies cannot tell night from day.\n\nThe encouraging news is that, unlike many forms of pollution, this one can be reversed almost instantly. Shielded lamps that point downward, timers on advertising boards, and simply switching off what we are not using could return the stars to our cities. The darkness, it turns out, is worth protecting.\n\nRead Passage P2. Why does the writer open by asking readers to \"ask your grandparents\"?",
